1.16 Steps vs. statements

  /* This code sets the sashelp.class table into a
   dataset called myclass, calculates height in cm,
   and also provides means */

data myclass;
    set sashelp.class;
    heightcm=height*2.54;
run;

proc print data=myclass;
run;

proc means data=myclass;
    var age heightcm;
run;

    This program has 3 steps (1 DATA step and 2 PROC steps).
    Most statements (not steps) begin with a keyword, and all statements end with a semicolon.

2.3 SAS libraries
A SAS library is a collection of SAS files that are stored in the same folder or directory.
Typing the full file path every time is long and easy to get wrong:
proc contents data="/home/u63936157/ow_hero.sas7bdat";
run;

Instead, we can store our data in a SAS library for ease of access. This is done with the global statement LIBNAME.

2.7 The work library

work is SAS’s scratch space. Every session starts with a fresh, empty one.
Any table you create without a libref goes there: data dates; is really data work.dates;
Use it for intermediate steps you don’t need to keep.
Everything in work is deleted when you sign out. To keep a table, save it with a permanent libref instead: data ma505.dates;

2.11 Your turn: get the Overwatch data into SAS

Download overwatch_heros.csv from Moodle.
In SAS Studio, open Server Files and Folders, right-click Files (Home) → Upload Files, and upload the file.
Right-click the uploaded file → Properties to see its full path, e.g. /home/u12345678/overwatch_heros.csv.
Create the ma505 library, then import the CSV into it as a SAS table (use your own user ID):


/* point the libref ma505 at your home folder */
libname ma505 "/home/u63936157";

/* import the CSV into the library as a SAS table called "overwatch" */
proc import datafile="/home/u63936157/overwatch_heros.csv"
    out=ma505.overwatch
    dbms=csv
    replace;
run;

3.2 Warm-up
Determine the number of steps, what steps, and how many statements are in this SAS code.
data work.class_summary;
    set sashelp.class;
    BMI = (Weight / (Height**2)) * 703;
    if BMI &gt;= 25 then Category = "Overweight";
    else Category = "Normal";
run;

proc sort data=work.class_summary;
    by descending BMI;
run;

proc print data=work.class_summary label;
    var Name Age Height Weight BMI Category;
    label BMI = "Body Mass Index"
          Category = "Weight Category";
    title "BMI Summary from SASHELP.CLASS";
run;





3 steps: 1 DATA step (lines 1–6), then 2 PROC steps: PROC SORT and PROC PRINT.
14 statements: 6 in the DATA step, 3 in PROC SORT, 5 in PROC PRINT.
The label statement spans two lines but is one statement: a statement ends at the semicolon, not at the end of the line.

3.3 Exploring data with PROCedures
Four procedures for a first look at any table: PRINT, MEANS, UNIVARIATE, and FREQ.
/* prints n rows for the listed columns */
proc print data=input-table(obs=n);
    var col-name(s);
run;

/* summary statistics for the listed columns */
proc means data=input-table;
    var col-name(s);
run;

/* summary statistics, with more about the distribution */
proc univariate data=input-table;
    var col-name(s);
run;

/* frequency table for each listed column */
proc freq data=input-table;
    tables col-name(s);
run;

3.12 More WHERE operators

AND / OR: combine expressions
where Type = "SUV" and MSRP &lt;= 30000;
IN / NOT IN: match any value in a list
where Type in ("SUV", "Truck");
IS MISSING / IS NOT MISSING
where Cylinders is missing;
BETWEEN … AND: an inclusive range
where MSRP between 36000 and 50000;
Dates: use a SAS date constant (Day 2)
where Date &gt; "01NOV2016"d;


Character values are case-sensitive: "suv" does not match "SUV".

3.15 SAS macro variables
A macro variable is a placeholder: a name that stores a value you can reuse throughout a program. It makes code dynamic, reusable, and flexible: change the value once, and every place that uses it changes too.

proc print data=sashelp.cars;
    where Make = "Kia";
    var Type Make Model MSRP;
run;

proc freq data=sashelp.cars;
    where Make = "Kia";
    tables Origin;
run;

/* set a macro variable */
%let CarMake = Kia;

proc print data=sashelp.cars;
    where Make = "&CarMake";
    var Type Make Model MSRP;
run;

proc freq data=sashelp.cars;
    where Make = "&CarMake";
    tables Origin;
run;


%let creates it; & uses it. Use double quotes: "&CarMake" works, '&CarMake' does not.
